[
    {
        "content": "<p>The overlapping instance linter is complaining about FLT having both <code>[IsTopologicalRing R]</code> and (typically later on in the file, when needed) <code>[NonarchimedeanRing R]</code>. The warning is</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">⚠️</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsTopologicalRing</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">and</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NonarchimedeanRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">each</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">imply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ContinuousAdd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ContinuousMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">and</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ContinuousNeg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`.</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">💡️</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">these</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsTopologicalRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">may</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">be</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">removed</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Everything here is a <code>Prop</code>. Should the linter be flagging this? The situation I'm faced with now is that I have loads of variables (rings, modules, algebras) in a section, an assumption <code>[IsTopologicalRing R]</code>, and then later on I need the stronger assumption <code>[NonarchimedeanRing R]</code> and I can't now add it without the warnings (which cause the build to fail), and I also can't omit <code>[IsTopologicalRing R]</code> because it's a section variable, so I have to close the section, re-initialise all my rings, modules and algebras, re-open everything, drop <code>[IsTopologicalRing R]</code> and add <code>[NonarchimedeanRing R]</code>. Is this what we want or is this unexpected behaviour?</p>",
        "id": 598714996,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1780144187
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It seems to me a good warning to have. Of course if it is too annoying in your case you can just deactivate the linter. A better option would be the possibility of turning of the warnings for <code>Prop</code> valued field.</p>",
        "id": 598715168,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1780144328
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If the overlaps are for Prop valued things, can this ever cause a problem? If not, then we should probably change the linter to not complain about them</p>",
        "id": 598715332,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1780144479
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The overlap is bad if it leads to lemmas having both assumptions <code>[IsTopologicalRing R]</code> and <code>[NonarchimedeanRing R]</code>  when the latter would be enough, as it means Lean will need to work more to apply the lemmas, for no good reason. I think the warning is definitely useful here, and refactoring your sections is the way to go.</p>",
        "id": 598715615,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1780144787
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Linters aren't just for problems, e.g. the unused variable linter will complain about <code>example (h : 1 = 1) : 1 = 1 := rfl</code> since there's an unnecessary assumption.<br>\nIt's great that it alerts the user about strange things going on in their theorem, even if TC-search will sort it out automatically.</p>",
        "id": 598715649,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1780144819
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110050\">Sébastien Gouëzel</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/overlapping.20instance.20linter/near/598715615\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The overlap is bad if it leads to lemmas having both assumptions</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Aah, I see! So this is the point. I will refactor. Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 598715713,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1780144908
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I also ran into the issue of <code>omit</code> not working when making adaptations to the linter in mathlib. See <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/.60omit.60.20doesn.27t.20work\">#lean4 &gt; &#96;omit&#96; doesn't work</a></p>",
        "id": 598719376,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1780148346
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"246273\">Bhavik Mehta</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/overlapping.20instance.20linter/near/598715332\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If the overlaps are for Prop valued things, can this ever cause a problem? If not, then we should probably change the linter to not complain about them</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Note, the linter only fires on Prop overlaps in situations like this: where a prop class is fully redundant, and can be synthesized from other instances in the context. It does not fire on strict overlaps of prop classes (since of course these pose no issue, stylistic or otherwise).</p>",
        "id": 598721982,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Murrills",
        "timestamp": 1780150662
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I found what I think is a false-positive:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>def</code>s do not automatically include section variables, so <code>foo</code> has only one <code>Nonempty</code> assumption which is fine.<br>\nRemoving the <code>Nonempty</code> section variable makes <code>foo_eq</code> error, and removing <code>Nonempty α</code> from <code>foo</code> makes the warning move to <code>foo_eq</code>. Removing both <code>Nonempty</code>s fixes the warnings.</p>\n<p>I think the linter should not warn here, though this is probably only an issue with code with <code>sorry</code>s.</p>",
        "id": 599275416,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1780358517
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why can't you remove the <code>Nonempty</code> from <code>def foo</code>?</p>",
        "id": 599276045,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1780358674
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Without the sorry, removing the <code>Nonempty</code> from <code>def foo</code> works fine</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">arbitrary</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>but with the sorry, there's a warning on <code>foo_eq</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">warning: automatically included section variable(s) unused in theorem `foo_eq`:</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  [Nonempty α]</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">consider restructuring your `variable` declarations so that the variables are not in scope or explicitly omit them:</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  omit [Nonempty α] in theorem ...</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">Note: This linter can be disabled with `set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false`</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 599276316,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1780358717
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"479299\">Jovan Gerbscheid</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/overlapping.20instance.20linter/near/599276045\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why can't you remove the <code>Nonempty</code> from <code>def foo</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It doesn't fix the warning, it moves to <code>foo_eq</code></p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"246273\">Bhavik Mehta</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/overlapping.20instance.20linter/near/599276316\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Without the sorry, removing the <code>Nonempty</code> from <code>def foo</code> works fine</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yeah, this is only an issue in development (or in downstream code)</p>",
        "id": 599276531,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1780358753
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/overlapping.20instance.20linter/near/599276531\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It doesn't fix the warning, it moves to <code>foo_eq</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It does fix the warning. The new warning on <code>foo_eq</code> is from a different linter altogether.</p>",
        "id": 599276866,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1780358818
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes but that's an issue. Imagine that I know <code>foo</code> is going to need <code>Nonempty</code> but Lean doesn't know it yet: I still need the proofs to assume <code>Nonempty α</code>, and I might want to make them do so before I fix <code>foo</code>.</p>",
        "id": 599278589,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1780359080
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I also found a way to make <code>foo</code> have zero <code>Nonempty</code> assumptions but the linter still complains:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>This is less of a problem because this is silly code, but it's still strange that the linter message says \"there are 2 <code>Nonempty</code> instances\" but there are actually none</p>",
        "id": 599279076,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1780359159
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ideally, <code>include</code> would be able to help you. Sadly this isn't even valid syntax with type class variables:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">include</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- syntax error</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 599279894,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1780359289
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/overlapping.20instance.20linter/near/599279076\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>because this is silly code</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is why the linter complains. This kind of pattern was surprisingly common in mathlib, which are now all removed thanks to the linter :)</p>",
        "id": 599280473,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1780359405
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The claim about there being 2 <code>Nonempty</code> instances is true in the local context in which foo is elaborated. This is kind of confusing and deserves a warning I think.</p>",
        "id": 599280875,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1780359515
    }
]